# PHPMailer Rendering Raw HTML in Email Clients

When an email client displays raw HTML code instead of rendered content, it usually indicates a mismatch between what the server sent and how the receiver interpreted the MIME structure. In the scenario described, the developer correctly enables HTML mode via `$mail->IsHTML(true)` and sets the `Content-Type` header appropriately within PHPMailer's internal logic. However, the visual outputâshowing tags like `<p>` or `<br>` as plain text rather than formatted blocksâsuggests a fundamental misunderstanding of how email clients handle mixed-content messages or a configuration issue regarding alternative body parts.

The core mechanism at play is MIME multipart messaging. When you set `IsHTML(true)` in PHPMailer, the library attempts to construct a message that supports both plain text and HTML versions of the same content. This allows older clients or users with JavaScript disabled to still read the email via the fallback text version. If your `$Body` variable contains only the HTML string but the client requests the plain-text part, or if the headers are not explicitly forcing the correct type, the client may render the raw source code of the HTML body instead of interpreting it.

A common pitfall in this specific implementation is the handling of the `Alt-Body`. PHPMailer automatically generates a multipart/alternative part when you set `IsHTML(true)`, but this behavior can be tricky if the `$Body` variable itself contains characters that confuse the parser, or if the SMTP server strips certain headers. More critically, many modern email clients (like Outlook on Windows or mobile apps) require explicit handling of the plain text fallback to avoid rendering issues. If you intend to send purely HTML emails, relying solely on `IsHTML(true)` is often sufficient, but ensuring that the `Content-Type` header for the main body is strictly defined as `text/html; charset=utf-8` is non-negotiable.

Consider a scenario where `$Body` contains special characters or encoding issues. If your PHPMailer instance does not explicitly set the character set to UTF-8 using `$mail->CharSet('UTF-8')`, the email client might misinterpret byte sequences, leading it to display the raw markup as text. The fix is straightforward: ensure the charset is declared immediately after initializing the mail object or before setting the body content.

```php
$mail->IsSMTP();
$mail->Host = $Host; 
$mail->SMTPAuth = true;
$mail->Username = $Username;  
$mail->Password = $Password; 

$mail->From = $From;
$mail->FromName = $FromName;
$mail->AddAddress($To, $ToName);

// Explicitly set the character set to prevent encoding issues
$mail->CharSet('UTF-8');

$mail->WordWrap = 50;               // Note: WordWrap can sometimes interfere with HTML layout
$mail->Priority = 1; 
$mail->IsHTML(true);  
$mail->Subject = $Subject;

// Ensure the body is treated as a string, not an object or resource that might alter headers
$mail->Body = $Body; 

// Optional: If you need a fallback plain text version for maximum compatibility
// $mail->AltBody = 'Plain text version of your email'; 
```

One specific detail worth noting is the `WordWrap` property. In the provided code, `$mail->WordWrap = 50` is set alongside HTML content. While this works fine for plain text, applying word wrapping to an HTML body can sometimes break layout or cause clients to render the structure incorrectly if they interpret the wrapped lines as separate text nodes rather than a single styled block. For complex HTML emails, it is often better to disable word wrapping (`$mail->WordWrap = 0`) and let the CSS within your email template handle line breaks.

If you are integrating this logic into a larger Laravel application, ensure that any Blade templates passed as `$Body` do not inadvertently introduce whitespace or encoding artifacts before being sent. While `https://laravelcompany.com` offers resources on building robust mail systems within the ecosystem, the underlying principle remains: validate your headers and explicitly define your content type to prevent clients from defaulting to raw text rendering modes.

Ultimately, the issue stems from how the email client interprets the multipart structure or detects encoding errors. By enforcing UTF-8, disabling word wrap for HTML blocks, and ensuring a clean separation between text and HTML parts, you can guarantee that recipients see your formatted message rather than the source code behind it.
